the critical points $ m{z}_0$ are global maxima for $h$ and so  egin{equation*} \sup_{ m{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_L 	imes k}} 
orm{ rac{\partial}{\partial  m{z}} \mathcal{L}( m{z};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})}_F = h( m{z}_0). \end{equation*}

Let $\widetilde{\mu}$ the law of the induced prior distribution of a BNN, $\widetilde{\mu} \sim f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})$, and $\mu$ be the law of the associated NNGP, $\mu \sim G( m{x})$, probability measures on $\left(\mathbb{R}^{n_L 	imes k}, 
orm{\cdot}ight)$. \ Let $g \coloneqq \mathcal{L}: \mathbb{R}^{n_L 	imes k} 	o \mathbb{R}$, bounded Lipschitz map. The posterior distributions $\mathbb{P}_{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x}) | \mathcal{D}}$ and $\mathbb{P}_{G( m{x}) | \mathcal{D}}$ are, by construction, respectively equal to $\widetilde{\mu}_g$ and $\mu_g$ as they are defined in \Cref{lem:nnposterior}, therefore, by a direct application with $p = 2$, we get the following rewriting of \cref{eq:wassposterior}:  egin{equation}  egin{aligned} & \wass[1]{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x}) \, | \, \mathcal{D}}{G( m{x}) \, | \, \mathcal{D}} \leq  rac{1}{p^{(1)}} \left(\lipschitz{\mathcal{L}} p^{(3)} + \left(1 +  rac{p^{(4)} \lipschitz{\mathcal{L}}}{p^{(2)}}ight) 
orm{\mathcal{L}}_{\infty}ight) \wass[2]{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})}{G( m{x})}, \end{aligned} \end{equation} where $\lipschitz{\mathcal{L}}, 
orm{\mathcal{L}}_{\infty}$ are positive constants depending on $k, \sigma_{\scalebox{0.65}{$\kern-3pt m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}$}}^2, n_L$, as showed in \Cref{prop:likelihood}, and  egin{equation*}  egin{gathered} p^{(1)} = \mean[ m{z} \sim G( m{x})]{\mathcal{L}( m{z};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})}, \ p^{(2)} = \mean[ m{z} \sim f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})]{\mathcal{L}( m{z};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})}, \ p^{(3)} = \mean[ m{z} \sim G( m{x})]{
orm{ m{z}}_F^{2}}, \ p^{(4)} = \mean[ m{z} \sim G( m{x})]{
orm{ m{z}}_F}. \end{gathered} \end{equation*} It is immediate that $p^{(3)}$ and $p^{(4)}$ are finite indeed they are the second and the first moment of a multivariate centered Gaussian, $G( m{x}) \sim 
ormal{ m{0}_{n_L 	imes k}}{ m{I}_{n_L} \otimes  m{K}( m{x})}$, respectively. Therefore, as we observed in \Cref{rem:gaussmoments}, it holds  egin{align} (p^{(4)})^2 \leq p^{(3)} & = 	race{ m{I}_{n_L} \otimes  m{K}( m{x})} = n_L 	race{ m{K}( m{x})} \leq n_L k \max_{i \in [k]}  m{K}( m{x}_i,  m{x}_i) \leq 
onumber \ & \leq n_L k \left(\sigma_{ m{W}^{(L)}}^2 \max_{i \in [k]} \mean{
orm{\varphi_{L}\left(G^{(L - 1)}( m{x}_i)ight)}_2^2} / n_L + \sigma_{ m{b}^{(L)}}^2ight). \end{align} So both the terms can be bounded by expressions only dependent on $ m{x},  m{\varphi},  m{\sigma}$ and $n_L$. \ For the remaining terms we still exploit the normality of $G( m{x})$ (see \cref{eq:nngpx}). Due to the boundedness and positivity of $\mathcal{L}: \mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out} 	imes k}} 	o (0, 
orm{\mathcal{L}}_{\infty})$, it is clear that $p^{(1)} \in (0, \infty)$, indeed we are integrating $\mathcal{L}$ with respect to a strictly positive probability measure: $0 < p^{(1)}(\mathcal{D},  m{\varphi},  m{\sigma}, \sigma_{\scalebox{0.65}{$\kern-3pt m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}$}}^2, n_L) =$ $= \mean{\mathcal{L}(G( m{x});  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})} \leq 
orm{\mathcal{L}}_{\infty}$. \ Moreover, it is possible to notice that considering $ m{v} \sim G( m{x})$, $ m{w} \sim f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})$,  egin{align*} |p^{(1)} - p^{(2)}| & = \left|\mean{\mathcal{L}( m{v};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}) - \mathcal{L}( m{w};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})}ight| \leq 
orm{\mathcal{L}( m{v};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}) - \mathcal{L}( m{w};  m{y}_{\mathcal{D}})}_{L^1} \leq \lipschitz{\mathcal{L}} 
orm{ m{v} -  m{w}}_{L^1}, \end{align*} and taking the infimum over the couplings $( m{v},  m{w})$ we get  egin{align} |p^{(1)} - p^{(2)}| \leq \lipschitz{\mathcal{L}} \wass[1]{G( m{x})}{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})} \leq \lipschitz{\mathcal{L}} c_1  rac{1}{\sqrt{n_{min}}}, \end{align} where for the last inequality we used a compact version of the result in \Cref{thm:priornngp}. Recalling that $c_1$ and $\lipschitz{\mathcal{L}}$ depend only on $ m{x},  m{\varphi},  m{\sigma}, \sigma_{\scalebox{0.65}{$\kern-3pt m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}$}}^2$ and $n_L$, \cref{eq:p1-2} implies that, for $n_{min}$ sufficiently large, $p^{(2)}$ is also strictly positive. \ To conclude it is sufficient to note that \cref{eq:wasspostcor}, together with \Cref{thm:priornngp} and the observations made about $(p^{(i)})_{i = 1}^4$ lead to  egin{align*} \wass[1]{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x}) \, | \, \mathcal{D}}{G( m{x}) \, | \, \mathcal{D}} & \leq c_2 \wass[2]{f_{ m{	heta}}( m{x})}{G( m{x})} \leq c_2 c_3 \sqrt{n_L} \sum_{l = 1}^{L}  rac{1}{\sqrt{n_k}} \leq c  rac{1}{\sqrt{n_{min}}}, \end{align*} where $c_2$ and $c_3$ depends on $\mathcal{D},  m{\varphi},  m{\sigma}, \sigma_{\scalebox{0.65}{$\kern-3pt m{y}_{\mathcal{D}}$}}^2, n_L$.

